3074 0 obj
  << /ActualText (Section 2\072 What Does A PBL Module Look Like\077\n\nIn project\055based learning\054 educators adopt a dynamic role\056 Instead of acting as traditional instructors\054 they fluidly transition between being the primary source of information for a project and serving as facilitators and guides throughout its progression\056 They also ensure that the goals students set at the project\047s onset are met\054 learning targets are appropriately integrated\054 and any questions that arise are sufficiently answered\056\240\n\nSound different from what you\047re used to\077 That\047s because PBL encourages students to take ownership of their learning\056 It\047s hands\055on training for the real world\054 where you\047ll have to think critically and make important decisions for yourself and your team\054 regardless of the occupation\056\240\240\n\nOne of the most important things you need to know about our modules is that they come in two forms\072 Teacher\055led Modules and Personal Projects\056\240\n\nTeacher\055led Modules \050TLM\051\n\nTeacher\055led Modules are the first type of PBL module at TGS\056 In a teacher\055led module\054 you\047ll work in teams to gather facts\054 look for correlations\054 and evaluate possible solutions for projects designed by our educators and based around your host country\056 Besides developing the module\054 educators act as your academic advisor and help steer the course across the eight\055week term\056\240\n\nDuring the first couple of weeks of each teacher\055led module\054 you\047ll look at the driving question from all angles and determine how you\047d like to approach it\056 Through this process\054 you\047ll decide on a final product that will be presented during the end\055of\055term showcase\054 where you and your teammates will demonstrate your learning by presenting before a live audience\056 \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n)
     /K [ 0 ]
     /P 3075 0 R
     /Pg 1484 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

