3112 0 obj
  << /ActualText (The Project Proposal\n\nThe project proposal serves as a roadmap\054 outlining the elements needed\054 including a title\054 the driving question\054 relevant background information or context\054 and specific objectives and learning targets\056\n\nAdditionally\054 the proposal includes the methodology and research sources to be used and a timeline with milestones and deadlines\056 It also lists the materials and resources needed and sets out assessment criteria for evaluating the project\047s success\056\nFurthermore\054 the proposal addresses anticipated challenges along with strategies to overcome them\054 describes the expected end product\054 and outlines plans for reflection on learning progress\056\n\nA critical step is presenting this proposal to a student\047s academic advisor\056 This presentation allows students to explain the project\047s components and gain valuable feedback and guidance\054 ensuring a well\055thought\055out and structured approach to achieving their goals\056\n\nThe Final Product\n\nIn project\055based learning\054 the final product is the tangible outcome that students create\054 representing the culmination of their learning process\056 The form of the final product can vary widely depending on the proposal and the nature of the project\056 It can range from research papers\054 presentations\054 and websites to more creative outputs such as songs\054 poems\054 short stories\054 or physical items like clothing\054 shoes\054 or models\056\n\nAt THINK Global School\054 showcasing these final products is integral to the learning process\056 Students present their work at the end of every term\054 allowing them to communicate their understanding\054 share their learning journey\054 and receive feedback\056 This practice also provides an opportunity for assessment and reflection on students\047 skills and knowledge gained throughout the project\056\n)
     /K [ 0 ]
     /P 3113 0 R
     /Pg 2562 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
